  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Plenty of sunshine with comfortable humidity \nFri-Sat. Highs mainly in the 70s. Lows Fri night in the 40s and \n50s. \n\nLarge high pressure arrives from the west Friday, then moves\noverhead for Saturday. This will mean mainly dry weather with\nrelatively light winds. Expecting rather good radiational\ncooling conditions Friday night. Some of the colder locations\ncould fall into the mid 40s, while the more urban centers should\nbe 55-60.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chances for showers return late Saturday night\ninto Sunday. Dry and seasonable weather returns Monday. \n\nAs the high pressure system moves offshore Saturday, a shortwave \npushes through southern New England likely bringing a wave of\nshowers with it Saturday night into Sunday. Dewpoints surging to \nnear 70 degrees and PWATs climbing to near 2\" by Sunday morning \nindicate the possibility that some showers could have some\nheavier banding set up, especially south of the Mass Pike.\nInitial model guidance is hinting at rainfall totals upwards of \n0.75\", but we're not quite within range of the CAMs yet. \nCertainly something to keep an eye on.\n\nAnother ridge of high pressure should begin to move in sometime \nlater Sunday evening or Monday bringing mainly dry and\nseasonable weather for the start of the next work week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A quick bought of summer like heat is possible\ntowards the middle of next weak ahead of a cold front.\n\nGlobal guidance is suggesting an anomalously strong upper level \nridge builds by the middle of next week over the southeastern \nstates and Appalachian region. This would indicate southern New \nEngland returns to predominantly west-southwest flow, pushing \nwarmer air from the south into New England. Depending on how \nmuch mixing we can generate at the surface ahead of the next \ncold front, there is potential for high temperatures to reach \nback into the 80s for a day or two by the middle of next week.",
